# Shatter — Weapons

This file defines what the ship shoots. The ship itself is in `specs/ship.md`,
what a shot does when it lands is in `specs/collision.md`, and the keys that fire
are in `specs/controls.md`.

## The gun

The ship's gun fires small round bullets from its nose.

| Rule | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Collision radius | `BULLET_R` (`3`) |
| Launch position | The ship's nose: ahead of the ship's centre along its facing, and no further from it than `SHIP_R` (`14`) |
| Launch velocity | The ship's current velocity plus `MUZZLE_SPEED` (`520`) along the ship's facing |
| Lifetime | `BULLET_LIFE` (`1.5` seconds), after which the bullet is removed |
| On-screen limit | `MAX_BULLETS` (`4`) of the ship's bullets in flight at once. While four are live, firing adds none until one leaves. |
| Fire gate | `FIRE_INTERVAL_TICKS` (`22`) whole ticks between shots |

One press of the fire key takes one shot when the gate allows it. Holding the key
takes a shot every `FIRE_INTERVAL_TICKS` for as long as it is held and the cap
allows one, so a held key tops out at `TICK_HZ / FIRE_INTERVAL_TICKS` shots per
second of game time.

A bullet is pulled by the well, wraps at the edges, and is removed when it is
absorbed by the star's core or when it lands, as `specs/collision.md` states.

## The bullet trail

Behind every moving bullet a fading tail traces its recent path, so the way the
star bends a shot reads at a glance.

- It reads as one continuous streak, a smooth comet rather than a row of discrete
  dots.
- It tapers from the bullet: widest and brightest where it meets the bullet, then
  narrowing and fading to nothing at its oldest end.
- It spans a fixed slice of recent travel, the last `TRAIL_TICKS` (`18`) ticks of
  the bullet's motion, so its drawn length is proportional to the bullet's current
  speed.
- Across a wrap it follows the bullet to the opposite edge rather than smearing
  across the field, so no drawn part of it is further from the bullet than
  `TRAIL_TICKS` of the bullet's travel by the shortest wrapped separation.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}

## The torpedo

The ship carries a second weapon on its own key: a single guided torpedo on a slow
recharge, distinct from the gun's bullets above.

### The charge

The ship holds one torpedo charge, a number from `0` to `1`. A game begins with
the charge at `1`, ready to fire.

- Launching a torpedo spends the charge, taking it to `0`.
- The charge then rises **linearly** from `0` to `1` over `TORPEDO_RECHARGE`
  (`10` seconds) of game time, so it reads `0.5` at five seconds and `0.25` at two
  and a half.
- The torpedo key does nothing while the charge is below `1`.
- At most one torpedo is in flight at a time. While one is up, the key launches
  nothing whatever the charge reads.
- A respawn refills the charge to `1` and cancels any recharge in progress.

### The launch

A torpedo leaves the ship's nose, ahead of the ship's centre along its facing and
no further from it than `SHIP_R` (`14`), with its heading set to the ship's facing
at that moment. It is self-propelled and carries none of the ship's drift, so its
velocity at launch is `TORPEDO_SPEED` (`420`) along that heading and nothing
else.

### The flight

| Rule | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Collision radius | `TORPEDO_R` (`6`) |
| Speed | `TORPEDO_SPEED` (`420`), held constant whether or not it is turning |
| Lifetime | `TORPEDO_LIFE` (`3.5` seconds), after which a torpedo that has hit nothing is removed |

The well never pulls a torpedo, so it holds its own course straight through the
gravity the star exerts. It wraps at the field's edges carrying its speed, and it
is removed when it is absorbed by the star's core or when it lands, as
`specs/collision.md` states.

### The guidance

Every tick a torpedo looks for a target. A body is a candidate when it is a rock
or the saucer and its bearing from the torpedo lies within `TORPEDO_CONE`
(`15` degrees) of the torpedo's current heading, on either side, so the cone spans
`30` degrees and looks forward alone. Among the candidates the torpedo takes the
nearest by shortest wrapped distance.

With a target, the torpedo turns its heading toward that target's current position
at up to `TORPEDO_TURN` (`160` degrees per second), keeping its speed. With no
candidate this tick, it flies straight on its current heading. It re-evaluates
every tick, so it acquires, loses, and re-acquires targets over its flight, and a
body behind it is never acquired.
{{/if}}
